{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 847 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "6b22934402505b7a717db5e88e6623b97a1e8407cc76531ab60a779a118149b5" :edb/db-before-t 846 :edb/db-after-t 847 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534159 50 #inst "2026-10-06T06:05:59.669Z" 13194139534159 true] [17592186119462 1100 "If $f(x)=3x^2+9$, find $(f^{-1})'(x)$ as a function of $f^{-1}(x)$." 13194139534159 false] [17592186119462 1100 "If $f(x)=3x^2+9,$ find $(f^{-1})'(x)$ as a function of $f^{-1}(x).$" 13194139534159 true] [17592186119462 1145 "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is\n\n$$\ny = 3x^2+9\n$$\n\nand to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=3y^2+9\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have\n\n$$\ny=f^{-1}(x)\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm dx}\n$$\n\nbut the equation is already solved for $x$, so we compute\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dx}{\\textrm dy}\n$$\n\nand then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dx}{dy} &= \\frac{d}{dy}(3y^{2} + 9) \\\\\n\\frac{dx}{dy} &= 6y \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{1}{6y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of $f^{-1}(x)$. Since\n\n$$\ny=f^{-1}(x)\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}[f^{-1}(x)] &= (1)/(6f^{-1}(x)) \\\\\n(f^{-1})^{′}(x) &= (1)/(6f^{-1}(x))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534159 false] [17592186119462 1145 "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y = 3x^2+9,$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=3y^2+9\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y=f^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm dx},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\dfrac{ \\textrm dx}{\\textrm dy}$ and then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(3{y}^{2}+9) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =6y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{6y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of $f^{-1}(x).$ Since $y=f^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{f}^{-1}(x)] & =\\frac{1}{6{f}^{-1}(x)} \\\\ ({f}^{-1})^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{6{f}^{-1}(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534159 true] [17592186119463 1100 "Find $\\left(f^{-1}\\right)'(x)$ as a function of $f^{-1}(x)$ if $f(x) = \\cos \\left(x^2\\right) +6$." 13194139534159 false] [17592186119463 1100 "Find $\\left(f^{-1}\\right)'(x)$ as a function of $f^{-1}(x)$ if $f(x) = \\cos \\left(x^2\\right) +6.$" 13194139534159 true] [17592186119463 1145 "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is\n\n$$\ny = \\cos \\left(x^2\\right) +6\n$$\n\nand to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx = \\cos \\left(y^2\\right) + 6\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have\n\n$$\ny=f^{-1}(x)\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm dx}\n$$\n\nbut the equation is already solved for $x$, so we compute\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dx}{\\textrm dy}\n$$\n\nand then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dx}{dy} &= \\frac{d}{dy}[\\cos (y^{2}) + 6] \\\\\n\\frac{dx}{dy} &= -2y\\sin (y^{2}) \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= - (1)/(2y\\sin (y^{2})) \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= -\\frac{1}{2y}\\csc (y^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of $f^{-1}(x)$. Since\n\n$$\ny=f^{-1}(x)\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}[f^{-1}(x)] &= - (1)/(2(f^{-1}(x)))\\csc ((f^{-1}(x))^{2}) \\\\\n(f^{-1})^{′}(x) &= - (1)/(2(f^{-1}(x)))\\csc ((f^{-1}(x))^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534159 false] [17592186119463 1145 "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y = \\cos \\left(x^2\\right) +6,$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx = \\cos \\left(y^2\\right) + 6\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y=f^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm dx},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\dfrac{ \\textrm dx}{\\textrm dy}$ and then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2})+6] \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =-2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{2y}\\operatorname{csc}⁡({y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of $f^{-1}(x).$ Since $y=f^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{f}^{-1}(x)] & =-\\frac{1}{2({f}^{-1}(x))}\\operatorname{csc}⁡({({f}^{-1}(x))}^{2}) \\\\ ({f}^{-1})^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{2({f}^{-1}(x))}\\operatorname{csc}⁡({({f}^{-1}(x))}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534159 true] [17592186119903 1020 #edb/ref 17592186279444 13194139534159 true] [17592186119903 1020 #edb/ref 17592186279472 13194139534159 true] [17592186119903 1020 #edb/ref 17592186279479 13194139534159 true] [17592186119903 1020 #edb/ref 17592186279486 13194139534159 true] [17592186119903 1020 #edb/ref 17592186279493 13194139534159 true] [17592186119904 1020 #edb/ref 17592186279451 13194139534159 true] [17592186119904 1020 #edb/ref 17592186279458 13194139534159 true] [17592186119904 1020 #edb/ref 17592186279465 13194139534159 true] [17592186119904 1020 #edb/ref 17592186279500 13194139534159 true] [17592186119904 1020 #edb/ref 17592186279507 13194139534159 true] [17592186279444 1017 #edb/ref 17592186279445 13194139534159 true] [17592186279444 1089 "q-32630" 13194139534159 true] [17592186279444 1091 #edb/ref 